Dear Management Team,

As part of the proposed GTS-level RAG initiative, we evaluated two potential repositories for consolidating knowledge across all GTS sub-pods:

1. SharePoint Online – Document Library
2. Confluence – Pages/Knowledge Base

Considering the nature of our GTS knowledge, RAG requirements, metadata needs, governance, security and future scalability, I recommend using SharePoint Online as the primary System of Record for GTS RAG knowledge.

Why SharePoint Online is the preferred option

Our GTS knowledge is not limited to wiki-style information. It consists of structured SDLC artifacts such as:

- Pre-FSR / requirement documents
- Video recordings and meeting transcripts
- FSR
- FDD
- User Stories
- Technical designs and architecture documents
- Application/project reference documents
- Approved and superseded versions of documents

For an enterprise RAG solution, the quality of metadata and governance around these artifacts is as important as the document content itself.

SharePoint provides a strong foundation through:

- Structured metadata and custom columns
- Content types and standardized classification
- Document versioning
- Approval and lifecycle management
- Enterprise access control and permissions
- Microsoft 365 ecosystem integration
- Centralized document governance
- Better support for document-oriented SDLC artifacts

Importance of metadata for RAG

We should avoid relying only on semantic/vector similarity during retrieval.

For example, if a user asks:

"Explain the Trade Confirmation requirement for Application X."

The RAG pipeline should first be able to identify/filter:

- GTS Sub-Pod
- Application / Project
- Business Domain
- SDLC Stage
- Artifact Type
- Release
- Version
- Approval Status
- RAG Eligibility
- Security Classification

It can then perform hybrid retrieval using metadata + keyword search + vector search + reranking.

This will significantly improve retrieval accuracy and reduce the possibility of retrieving obsolete or unrelated information.

Proposed SharePoint structure

We recommend establishing a central:

GTS Knowledge Hub

with a standardized structure:

GTS Knowledge Hub
→ GTS Sub-Pod
→ Application / Project
→ Artifact Type

Example:

GTS Knowledge Hub
└── Payments
└── Trade Application
├── 01_Pre-FSR
├── 02_FSR
├── 03_FDD
├── 04_User-Stories
├── 05_Video-Transcripts
├── 06_Technical-Design
├── 07_Architecture
└── 08_Reference

The same structure should be adopted consistently across all GTS sub-pods.

Importantly, folders should provide intuitive navigation, while metadata should remain the primary mechanism for RAG classification and retrieval.

Proposed core metadata

We should establish a common GTS metadata taxonomy covering:

- GTS Sub-Pod
- Application / Project
- Business Domain
- SDLC Stage
- Artifact Type
- Requirement / User Story ID
- Release
- Version
- Status
- Approval Status
- Document Owner
- Technology
- Security Classification
- Effective Date
- Parent Artifact ID
- Source Type
- RAG Eligible
- RAG Priority
- Knowledge ID

This will allow the ingestion pipeline to carry enterprise context into the vector database.

Proposed RAG architecture

The target flow would be:

SharePoint
→ Metadata & Governance
→ Document/Transcript Extraction
→ Context-Aware Chunking
→ Metadata Enrichment
→ Embedding
→ Vector + Keyword Retrieval
→ Metadata Filtering
→ Reranking
→ GAIP RAG
→ Answer with Source/Citation

For video recordings, we should retain the recording in the approved repository while extracting the transcript, timestamps and relevant metadata into the RAG ingestion pipeline.

Role of Confluence

This recommendation does not mean Confluence cannot be used.

Confluence can continue to be valuable for wiki-style knowledge, team collaboration, FAQs, technical notes and continuously evolving knowledge.

However, where the objective is to establish a governed enterprise knowledge foundation for GTS RAG, SharePoint is the preferred primary repository.

Confluence can subsequently be integrated as an additional knowledge source if required.

Recommendation

I recommend that we:

1. Establish SharePoint Online as the primary GTS RAG Knowledge Repository / System of Record.
2. Define a common GTS metadata taxonomy before onboarding content.
3. Standardize the library/content structure across all sub-pods.
4. Establish document lifecycle and RAG eligibility rules.
5. Build the ingestion pipeline around metadata-aware processing.
6. Keep the architecture open to Confluence and other enterprise sources in the future.

This approach will provide a more governed, scalable and metadata-driven foundation for the GTS Enterprise RAG initiative and will also enable us to evolve towards more advanced capabilities such as knowledge graphs and SDLC relationship-based retrieval in the future.

